<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Four weeks of evening fevers and night sweats, 5 kg weight loss and right upper quadrant pain with a tender liver in a 29-year-old mathematician from Tamil Nadu, a strict vegetarian, who had dysentery twice in India years ago. Tuberculosis and an amebic or pyogenic liver abscess are the leading possibilities. Please help interpret the tests, decide the treatment and its order, and advise on any tuberculosis infection found. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within 48 hours, on the day of his second visit, when the ultrasound and first results are back. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do the results point to an amebic liver abscess, a pyogenic abscess, or tuberculosis?</item><item>Is active tuberculosis excluded well enough to treat the liver without tuberculosis drugs while cultures are pending?</item><item>Does the abscess need aspiration or drainage?</item><item>Which drugs, in what order and for how long?</item><item>If the IGRA is positive, should latent tuberculosis infection be treated, and when, given the liver disease?</item><item>What follow-up, imaging and public health steps are needed?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Ramanujan, 29, is seen at the request of Dr. Ashworth two days after his first visit. He prefers to be called Mr. Ramanujan. He describes four weeks of evening fevers and sweats and three weeks of a dull right upper quadrant ache, worse on deep breathing and lying on his right side, with poor appetite and weight loss. He had dysentery twice in South India many years ago. He has not had diarrhea in years, has not traveled since he arrived three years ago, has no known tuberculosis contact and has never been tested for tuberculosis. No cough with sputum or blood. He keeps a strict vegetarian diet and does not drink alcohol. He is frightened that he has tuberculosis and asked to be told plainly. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Srinivasa Ramanujan was born on 22 December 1887 in his grandmother&apos;s house in Erode, in what is now Tamil Nadu, and grew up in Kumbakonam. His father, Kuppuswamy Srinivasa, was a clerk in a cloth merchant&apos;s shop, and his mother, Komalatammal, sang devotional songs; the family was Brahmin and poor. [D]</item><item>He had no family name in the Western sense. In Tamil usage the father&apos;s given name comes first as an initial or prefix, so &quot;Srinivasa&quot; is his father&apos;s name and &quot;Ramanujan&quot; is his own given name, the name by which he was known and addressed. [D]</item><item>He had smallpox in December 1889, fell ill after about three months at a college in Madras in 1906, and had a serious illness in 1908 followed by an operation in 1909 and a long recovery. [D]</item><item>He had two episodes of dysentery in India, about eleven and eight years before his illness in England. [D]</item><item>He married Janaki in July 1909, worked as a clerk at the Madras Port Trust from March 1912, wrote to G. H. Hardy at Cambridge in January 1913, sailed for England in March 1914, and moved into rooms at Trinity College, Cambridge, at the end of April 1914. [D]</item><item>An orthodox Brahmin, he was a strict vegetarian. He had trouble with his diet from the start in England; the war that began a few months after his arrival made the foods he needed harder to get, and he cooked for himself, sometimes only once a day or every other day. [D]</item></list><paragraph>9 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

